From 5153c443ffc64f7c6b7f2da825ced7f2cdb2acec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:16:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add rule literal_no_separator

ABAP requires a separator after a closing literal; the lexer ends the literal
at its delimiter and starts the next token right there, so `m( a = `x`b = `y` ).`
parses here and is refused by a system with "There must be a space or
equivalent character (":", ",", ".") after `x`." Two such lines reached a
released abap2UI5/samples-controls main and were found four weeks later by a
user's Code Inspector run.

The rule walks adjacent token pairs per statement and reports a StringToken,
StringTemplate or StringTemplateEnd that ends in the same column the next
token starts in, when that token begins with a name character. What may
legally follow a literal stays unreported: the text symbol 'text'(001) opens
a parenthesis rather than a name character, a doubled delimiter is not a
closing one, and a symbol - `x`. `x`) `x`&&`y` - is not the error the
compiler's message is about.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
